  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1206 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nDry weather will return for Sunday with temperatures remaining below \nnormal for most of the region.  The next upper trough moves into the \nwestern U.S. Sunday night and will spread snow back into the \nContinental Divide on Monday and Monday night as it moves across \nColorado as another open wave. Isolated to scattered showers may \nspill eastward onto the plains Monday night, but overall, any \nsnow or precipitation amounts will remain light given the \nprogressive nature of the system. Another 1 to 3 inches of snow\nwill be possible along the Continental Divide, with a spotty \ninch or two across the remainder of the mountains. The plains \ncould see a light dusting of snow overnight, though this will \nbe localized and most areas may stay dry. \n\nDry weather with a gradual warming trend will return for the mid \nweek time frame, then models diverge with the timing, amplitude and \ntrack of the next system over the Thanksgiving holiday. \nCanadian and EC and their ensembles bring another open trough \nacross CO in the Wed/Thu timeframe, while GFS takes the majority\nof the energy/moisture much farther south into Old Mexico. GFS \nEnsemble means were more in line with the Canadian and EC. Given\nuncertainties, unsettled weather will continue with another \nround of mountain snows possible along the Continental Divide \nsometime late week. Forecast for the southeast plains leans dry\nfor now with temperatures around seasonal averages. -KT",
